<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>Fire Safety Management System (ГДПБЗН) — Documentation</title>
<style>
  :root{
    --navy:#15334f; --navy-dark:#091a29; --red:#c1271d;
    --ink:#1b2430; --muted:#5d6875; --border:#d3dae2; --soft:#eef1f5;
  }
  *{box-sizing:border-box;}
  body{
    font-family:"Segoe UI",system-ui,-apple-system,Arial,sans-serif;
    color:var(--ink); line-height:1.6; max-width:900px; margin:0 auto;
    padding:48px 28px 80px; background:#fff;
  }
  header.doc-head{border-bottom:3px solid var(--red); padding-bottom:20px; margin-bottom:8px;}
  header.doc-head h1{font-size:30px; margin:0 0 6px; color:var(--navy-dark); letter-spacing:-.01em;}
  header.doc-head .sub{color:var(--muted); font-weight:600;}
  header.doc-head .meta{color:var(--muted); font-size:14px; margin-top:6px;}
  h2{font-size:22px; color:var(--navy); margin:38px 0 12px; padding-bottom:6px; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);}
  h3{font-size:17px; color:var(--navy); margin:24px 0 8px;}
  p{margin:10px 0;}
  a{color:var(--navy); }
  code{background:var(--soft); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:4px; padding:1px 5px; font-family:Consolas,"Courier New",monospace; font-size:13.5px;}
  pre{background:var(--navy-dark); color:#e8eef4; border-radius:8px; padding:16px 18px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13.5px; line-height:1.5;}
  pre code{background:none; border:none; color:inherit; padding:0;}
  table{border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; margin:14px 0; font-size:14.5px;}
  th,td{border:1px solid var(--border); padding:9px 12px; text-align:left; vertical-align:top;}
  th{background:var(--soft); color:var(--navy); text-transform:uppercase; font-size:12px; letter-spacing:.04em;}
  tbody tr:nth-child(even){background:#f7f9fb;}
  ul{margin:10px 0; padding-left:22px;}
  li{margin:5px 0;}
  .note{border-left:4px solid var(--red); background:#fbeae8; padding:12px 16px; border-radius:0 6px 6px 0; margin:16px 0; font-size:14.5px;}
  footer{margin-top:48px; padding-top:16px; border-top:1px solid var(--border); color:var(--muted); font-size:13px;}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<header class="doc-head">
  <h1>Fire Safety Management System (ГДПБЗН)</h1>
  <div class="sub">Project documentation</div>
  <div class="meta">Version 1.0 · Learning project · Web application for the daily operations of a fire department</div>
</header>

<h2>1. Overview</h2>
<p>The Fire Safety Management System is a web application that supports the operational work of a fire and rescue service. It covers the full cycle of handling an emergency: a citizen or operator registers a signal, the control room registers the incident and assigns a team, firefighters confirm participation and coordinate through tasks, resource requests and chat, and the dispatcher closes the incident and frees the team.</p>
<p>The system serves four kinds of users: an anonymous citizen, a dispatcher (control room), a firefighter, and an administrator. Each authenticated role sees a tailored interface and is restricted to the actions permitted for that role.</p>
<p>The user interface is in Bulgarian; the source code, comments and this documentation are in English.</p>

<h2>2. Technology stack</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Technology</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Language</td><td>Python 3</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Web framework</td><td>Flask (application factory + blueprints)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Templating</td><td>Jinja2</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Database</td><td>SQLite (single file, accessed through <code>sqlite3</code>)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Authentication</td><td>Werkzeug password hashing + Flask server-side sessions</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Front end</td><td>HTML, CSS, vanilla JavaScript</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Map</td><td>Leaflet + OpenStreetMap (incident GPS point and reverse geocoding)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Live chat</td><td>JavaScript polling of JSON endpoints</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>No heavy ORM or front-end framework is used; the application relies on thin SQL helper functions and server-rendered pages.</p>

<h2>3. System architecture</h2>

<h3>3.1 Request lifecycle</h3>
<ul>
  <li><code>run.py</code> calls <code>create_app()</code> and starts the development server.</li>
  <li><code>create_app()</code> (in <code>app/__init__.py</code>) configures the app, registers the database teardown hook, builds the database on first run, and registers all blueprints.</li>
  <li>An incoming HTTP request is routed to a view function inside a blueprint.</li>
  <li>The view reads or writes data through the helpers in <code>app/db.py</code> and renders a Jinja2 template from <code>app/templates/</code>.</li>
  <li>Static assets (CSS, JavaScript, the logo image) are served from <code>app/static/</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3.2 Application factory</h3>
<p><code>create_app()</code> performs the following steps in order:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Creates the Flask application and loads settings from <code>config.Config</code>.</li>
  <li>Registers <code>db.close_db</code> as a teardown handler so the SQLite connection is closed after every request.</li>
  <li>On first run (no <code>users</code> table), builds the database from <code>schema.sql</code> and <code>seed.sql</code>; on every run it idempotently ensures the demo vehicles and users exist.</li>
  <li>Registers the nine blueprints.</li>
  <li>Installs a context processor that injects <code>current_user</code> and the <code>role_labels</code> dictionary into every template.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3.3 Modules (blueprints)</h3>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Blueprint</th><th>Responsibility</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>public</code></td><td>Home page and the citizen incident-report form (no login).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>auth</code></td><td>Staff login and logout.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>dashboard</code></td><td>Role-specific overview and the operations channel.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>employees</code></td><td>Staff and vehicle management.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>incidents</code></td><td>Incidents: list, create, detail, team assignment, status.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>tasks</code></td><td>Tasks per incident.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>resources</code></td><td>Resource requests per incident.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>messages</code></td><td>Per-incident chat (post + JSON feed).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ops</code></td><td>Operations channel: shared staff / control-room chat.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>3.4 Database access layer</h3>
<p><code>app/db.py</code> exposes a minimal interface used by every module:</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>get_db()</code> — opens (and caches per request) a SQLite connection with row access by name and foreign keys enabled.</li>
  <li><code>query(sql, params, one)</code> — runs a SELECT and returns rows (or a single row).</li>
  <li><code>execute(sql, params)</code> — runs an INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE, commits, and returns the new row id.</li>
  <li><code>init_app(app)</code> / <code>close_db()</code> — tie the connection to the request lifecycle.</li>
</ul>

<h2>4. Directory structure</h2>
<pre><code>├── run.py                  # starts the server
├── config.py               # settings (database path, secret key)
├── requirements.txt        # libraries
├── database/
│   ├── schema.sql          # table definitions
│   ├── seed.sql            # base data (message templates, etc.)
│   ├── init_db.py          # optional manual rebuild
│   └── gdpbzn.db           # the SQLite database (generated)
├── app/
│   ├── __init__.py         # application factory + blueprint registration
│   ├── db.py               # database helper functions
│   ├── seed.py             # builds the database + demo data
│   ├── auth_utils.py       # login and access control (roles)
│   ├── chat_reads.py       # chat "seen" receipts
│   ├── blueprints/         # the modules (one file per topic)
│   ├── templates/          # HTML pages (Jinja2)
│   └── static/             # CSS, JS and the logo image
└── docs/
    └── diagrams/           # block schemes and the ER diagram (.drawio)</code></pre>

<h2>5. Roles and access control</h2>
<p>Access is enforced by two decorators in <code>app/auth_utils.py</code>:</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>login_required</code> — the user must be authenticated.</li>
  <li><code>role_required(*roles)</code> — the user must hold one of the listed roles.</li>
</ul>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Authentication</th><th>Main capabilities</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Citizen</td><td>None</td><td>Submit an incident report from the public page.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Dispatcher</td><td>Required</td><td>Register incidents, view staff statuses, assign the team, create tasks, approve resource requests, change incident status, chat.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Firefighter</td><td>Required</td><td>View own incidents and tasks, confirm participation, change own status (limited subset), per-incident chat, operations channel.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Administrator</td><td>Required</td><td>Manage staff and vehicles, assign roles.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>The signed-in user and a Bulgarian label for the role are made available to every template through a context processor.</p>

<h2>6. Functional modules</h2>

<h3>6.1 Public reporting</h3>
<p>The home page presents two clear actions: submit a signal or call 112. The report form captures incident type, address and an optional description, and stores the report as a new incident through the &quot;Онлайн&quot; channel.</p>

<h3>6.2 Incident management</h3>
<p>Dispatchers and administrators create incidents (type, channel, address, optional GPS point selected on a Leaflet map with reverse geocoding). The incident detail page shows the assigned team, tasks, resource requests, a map marker, and the incident chat. Staff can be assigned and unassigned; assigning a person sets their status to &quot;На произшествие&quot; and closing the incident returns the whole team to &quot;Наличен&quot;. When a firefighter is on a team, they can confirm that they are taking the case.</p>
<p>The assign dropdown groups available staff by category (commanders, firefighters, drivers, rescuers, others) and lists unavailable staff separately and disabled.</p>

<h3>6.3 Tasks and resources</h3>
<p>Tasks belong to an incident, have a category and a status that moves Нова → В процес → Приключена, and can be assigned to a team member. Resource requests belong to an incident and move Заявена → Одобрена → Доставена; any signed-in user can raise one, while only dispatchers and administrators advance the status.</p>

<h3>6.4 Staff and vehicles</h3>
<p>Administrators add and edit staff (name, username, password, role, position, phone, assigned vehicle) and manage the vehicle fleet. Dispatchers can view staff and change a person&#x2019;s status. Usernames are unique; passwords are stored only as hashes.</p>

<h3>6.5 Communication</h3>
<p>Two chat surfaces exist:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Per-incident chat</strong> (<code>messages</code>) — tied to a single incident.</li>
  <li><strong>Operations channel</strong> (<code>ops_messages</code>) — a shared staff / control-room channel not tied to any incident, shown on the dashboard.</li>
</ul>
<p>Both surfaces refresh live: the browser polls a JSON endpoint every few seconds and re-renders the message list. Read receipts are tracked in <code>chat_reads</code>, which records the highest message id each user has seen per channel and produces a &quot;seen by&quot; line. Ready-made message templates speed up common replies.</p>

<h2>7. Data model</h2>
<p>The database contains ten tables. The authoritative definition is in <code>database/schema.sql</code>; the visual model is in <code>docs/diagrams/er-diagram.drawio</code>.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Table</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Key references</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>vehicles</code></td><td>Fire vehicles.</td><td>—</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>users</code></td><td>Staff accounts and status.</td><td><code>vehicle_id → vehicles</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>incidents</code></td><td>Registered incidents.</td><td><code>created_by → users</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>incident_assignments</code></td><td>Staff assigned to an incident (the team).</td><td><code>incident_id → incidents</code>, <code>user_id → users</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>tasks</code></td><td>Tasks within an incident.</td><td><code>incident_id → incidents</code>, <code>assigned_to → users</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>resource_requests</code></td><td>Resource requests within an incident.</td><td><code>incident_id → incidents</code>, <code>requested_by → users</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>messages</code></td><td>Per-incident chat messages.</td><td><code>incident_id → incidents</code>, <code>user_id → users</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>message_templates</code></td><td>Ready-made chat replies.</td><td>— (standalone)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ops_messages</code></td><td>Operations-channel messages.</td><td><code>user_id → users</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>chat_reads</code></td><td>&quot;Seen&quot; receipts per channel and user.</td><td><code>user_id</code> references <code>users</code> (logical)</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Referential rules: assignments, tasks, resources and messages are deleted together with their incident (<code>ON DELETE CASCADE</code>); user references on incidents, tasks, resources and messages are nulled when a user is removed (<code>ON DELETE SET NULL</code>).</p>

<h2>8. Setup and running</h2>
<pre><code># 1. Install the libraries
pip install -r requirements.txt

# 2. Start the server (the database is created automatically on first run)
python run.py</code></pre>
<p>Then open <strong>http://127.0.0.1:5000</strong> in a browser.</p>
<p>To wipe and rebuild the database from scratch:</p>
<pre><code>python database/init_db.py</code></pre>

<h3>Demo accounts</h3>
<p>All demo accounts use the password <code>1234</code>.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Username</th><th>Role</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>admin</code></td><td>Administrator</td><td>Full staff, vehicle and role management.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>dispatcher</code></td><td>Dispatcher</td><td>Registers incidents, assigns teams, tasks, resources.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;surname&gt;</code></td><td>Firefighter</td><td>Any firefighter logs in with their surname in lowercase Latin (for example <code>ivanov</code>, <code>petrov</code>).</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>9. Security notes and limitations</h2>
<p>This is a learning project and is configured for local use, not production:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Passwords are stored as Werkzeug hashes; the shared demo password <code>1234</code> is for convenience only.</li>
  <li>The Flask <code>SECRET_KEY</code> falls back to a placeholder; set a real value via the <code>SECRET_KEY</code> environment variable before any real deployment.</li>
  <li>The server runs with <code>debug=True</code>, which must be disabled in production.</li>
  <li>The application has no CSRF protection and relies on SQLite, which is suitable for a single-user / classroom setting rather than a concurrent production load.</li>
  <li>All SQL uses parameterised queries, and user-supplied text is HTML-escaped before it is rendered into the chat, mitigating SQL injection and stored XSS in those paths.</li>
</ul>

<h2>10. Diagrams</h2>
<p>The <code>docs/diagrams/</code> folder contains the following draw.io files:</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>architecture.drawio</code> — application structure and request flow.</li>
  <li><code>incident-flow.drawio</code> — the lifecycle of an incident, from signal to closure.</li>
  <li><code>roles-access.drawio</code> — roles and the actions available to each.</li>
  <li><code>er-diagram.drawio</code> — the database entity-relationship diagram.</li>
</ul>

<footer>Fire Safety Management System (ГДПБЗН) · Project documentation · Version 1.0</footer>

</body>
</html>
